WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:03.200
Steam.
The largest game distributor on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:12.640
earth. 74% market share of PC gaming, 17 billion&nbsp;
in annual revenue, over a hundred million users.&nbsp;

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:19.680
And, it seems like everyone is suing them.
Lawsuits on top of lawsuits.&nbsp;

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:22.960
Accusations of being a&nbsp;
monopoly, and price steering.&nbsp;

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:27.280
With one seeking $900 million.
But, the weirdest part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:33.200
is that customers love Steam.
Despite being the big bad monopoly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:38.320
players go to war on Steam’s behalf.
Why do competitors seem to hate Steam,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:47.920
and customers love it?
Is Steam really a monopoly?&nbsp;

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:58.080
Steam is a gigantic digital store,&nbsp;
library, and game launcher used by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:05.280
hundreds of millions. It’s owned and run by Valve.
Steam has over 130 million monthly active users,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:10.000
69 million daily active users, and&nbsp;
has over 40 million concurrent users.&nbsp;

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:16.000
And they’re in a lot of hot water, especially&nbsp;
recently. With some serious accusations.&nbsp;

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:21.840
There are quite a few lawsuits involving Valve,&nbsp;
but there’s a series of cases circling one thing:&nbsp;

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:26.320
Steam breaching antitrust laws.
The most important part to understand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:33.760
is that, broadly, Valve takes 30% from all&nbsp;
sales on Steam. 30% of revenue. The same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:40.320
as Apple and Google Play, and GOG, while&nbsp;
Epic Games, the Microsoft Store take 12%.&nbsp;

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:44.160
But it’s not the only reason&nbsp;
for Valve’s many lawsuits.&nbsp;

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:49.680
One of the biggest is from Vicki Shotbolt&nbsp;
vs Valve, which was filed on behalf of 14&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:53.600
million UK Steam consumers.
The claim is seeking £656&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.600 --> 00:02:01.120
million, about $900 million.
Why? That 30% cut, which they call “excessive”..&nbsp;

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:06.320
But also, accusations of “anti-steering”.
A product bought on Steam with in-game&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:12.000
purchases must be done through Steam’s&nbsp;
API, and thus, Valve’s 30% cut.&nbsp;

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:18.720
But the bigger accusation is around price parity.
Prices on other platforms are the same as Steam.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:26.000
Sounds normal, but they have the platforms taking&nbsp;
a different cut. 30%, or 12, or something else.&nbsp;

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:32.080
But prices aren’t different because Valve&nbsp;
quietly enforces “Platform Parity Obligations”.&nbsp;

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:35.760
Which has caused another lawsuit&nbsp;
from developer: Wolfire Games&nbsp;

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:40.160
“On December 3, 2018, Steam account&nbsp;
manager reportedly told publisher&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:45.760
Wolfire Games that Steam would delist any&nbsp;
games available for a lower price elsewhere”&nbsp;

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:49.280
This included platforms where the&nbsp;
revenue split is lower, which could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:54.320
be the only reason for the lower price.
The core allegation is that “Valve uses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:59.680
its dominance over PC game distribution&nbsp;
to impose a 30% commission on nearly every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:03.920
sale and – critically – to enforce a&nbsp;
“Platform Most Favored Nation” rule&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:08.000
that the plaintiffs say prevents developers&nbsp;
from selling the same game more cheaply on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:14.400
competing stores or even on their own websites.”
“This 30% commission yields Valve over $6 billion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:19.600
dollars in annual revenue. For everyone else,&nbsp;
it yields higher prices and less innovation.”&nbsp;

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:24.000
[David Rosen, CEO of Wolfire]: "most developers&nbsp;
have little or no choice but to sell on Steam and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:28.560
do as they're told by Valve. I believe that&nbsp;
businesses are free to do whatever they want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:34.160
within the law. However, once a company reaches&nbsp;
a certain level of power over an entire market,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:37.920
the antitrust laws forbid those&nbsp;
companies from distorting competition.”&nbsp;

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:43.600
“As Valve itself told one game publisher, Valve&nbsp;
“can’t afford” to let competing platforms sell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:47.680
games more cheaply than Steam because&nbsp;
“[t]hat would only lead to our audience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:52.640
and the public thinking we are overpriced and&nbsp;
there are better alternatives to find games.”&nbsp;

00:03:52.640 --> 00:03:56.880
Valve denies enforcing this, though&nbsp;
files that have come out of this lawsuit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.880 --> 00:04:02.160
have brought even more problems, which&nbsp;
has also dragged Microsoft into this.&nbsp;

00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:06.640
A proposed class action filed in May&nbsp;
2026 alleges Microsoft entered an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:12.160
unlawful agreement with Valve requiring "price&nbsp;
parity" between the Microsoft Store and Steam.&nbsp;

00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:16.960
This was based on an internal Microsoft email,&nbsp;
where "one employee asks whether Steam requires&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.960 --> 00:04:23.120
price parity, and in response, another Microsoft&nbsp;
employee replied ‘Yes - they absolutely do…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:28.080
It’s not formally listed in documentation in&nbsp;
Steamworks, but always addressed in person.”&nbsp;

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:31.760
The complaint argues that “By choosing&nbsp;
to collude rather than compete,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.760 --> 00:04:37.360
Microsoft deprived consumers of the benefits&nbsp;
of competition: lower prices, increased output,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:42.320
unique content selection, and higher quality”
There are also accusations, that Valve has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.320 --> 00:04:46.320
reportedly threatened major publishers&nbsp;
such as Ubisoft with delisting games&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:50.960
like Rainbow Six Siege if they attempted to&nbsp;
offer lower prices on competing storefronts.&nbsp;

00:04:50.960 --> 00:04:55.360
In response, CEO Gabe Newell&nbsp;
denied any existence of such rules,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.360 --> 00:05:00.720
and said “ Valve does not have a policy or&nbsp;
practice of dictating prices to third-party&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:06.400
software developers on other platforms.”
That’s a lot of pretty strong accusations.&nbsp;

00:05:06.400 --> 00:05:11.200
And on the sidelines of pretty much every&nbsp;
lawsuit, is one of Valve’s most vocal critics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:18.240
Tim Sweeney, CEO of Epic Games. Creator of&nbsp;
the Unreal Engine, and most notably: Fortnite.&nbsp;

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:23.360
“Valve is the only major store still holding&nbsp;
onto the payments tie and 30% junk fee’&nbsp;

00:05:23.360 --> 00:05:28.480
He has quite a few tweets about Steam.
To be fair to Tim, he isn’t singling out Valve.&nbsp;

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:31.680
He went after Apple for&nbsp;
demanding 30% on the App Store.&nbsp;

00:05:32.640 --> 00:05:38.160
Steam and Valve seem to be the bad guys here.
But, this whole thing, is a bit more murky&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:42.160
than you might think.
Players, love, Steam.&nbsp;

00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:47.280
And they’re inherently untrusting of&nbsp;
all their competitors, including Tim.&nbsp;

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:52.960
So what makes Valve and Gabe Newell different?
Not only that, can’t so many,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.960 --> 00:05:59.360
sometimes bigger competitors, beat Steam?
Tim Sweeney’s Epic, EA, Microsoft, Amazon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.360 --> 00:06:06.800
Discord, Battle.net, Ubisoft, Humble, GOG and&nbsp;
Green Man Gaming, can’t seem to beat Steam.&nbsp;

00:06:06.800 --> 00:06:13.920
Even those with way, way more money.
What’s really going on here?&nbsp;

00:06:20.720 --> 00:06:26.560
If we just look at the accusations and the&nbsp;
lawsuits, it does seem like Valve is at fault.&nbsp;

00:06:26.560 --> 00:06:30.960
And, they very well might be. I’m not&nbsp;
saying they’ve done nothing wrong.&nbsp;

00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:34.640
But this gets weirder when you&nbsp;
look at what Steam represents.&nbsp;

00:06:34.640 --> 00:06:38.640
Steam is famous for its fantastic&nbsp;
value and constant sales.&nbsp;

00:06:38.640 --> 00:06:42.480
It’s not uncommon for your favourite&nbsp;
game, if it’s not brand new, to be on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.480 --> 00:06:49.920
sale for 50%, 70%, even in some cases 90% off.
Better deals than you can get anywhere else.&nbsp;

00:06:49.920 --> 00:06:55.520
And ironically, one of the best arguments&nbsp;
for Steam, actually comes from Epic.

00:06:55.520 --> 00:06:59.440
Epic Games has been trying to pull&nbsp;
players from Steam for a while.&nbsp;

00:06:59.440 --> 00:07:07.840
$2 billion in promotional spending, after 6&nbsp;
years, Epic Games Store has… 3% of the market.&nbsp;

00:07:07.840 --> 00:07:12.960
And did I mention that Epic has been&nbsp;
giving away free games to get new users?&nbsp;

00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:18.720
That’s right. Epic literally gives games&nbsp;
away for free, and people will still use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:25.920
Steam. Not only that, “Internal analyses&nbsp;
suggest only 16–18% of free-game claimers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.920 --> 00:07:30.480
ever purchase a title on the store.”
Massive games like Metro Exodus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.480 --> 00:07:35.600
Borderlands 3, and many other titles&nbsp;
are only on the Epic Game store.&nbsp;

00:07:35.600 --> 00:07:41.360
Metro was actually listed on Steam, before&nbsp;
being pulled right before launch, after Epic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.360 --> 00:07:45.120
paid the publisher to do so.
They also paid $146 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.120 --> 00:07:50.160
for Borderlands to be an exclusive.
Customers, weren’t happy. Ironically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.160 --> 00:07:54.000
when Borderlands 4 came out a few&nbsp;
years later, it returned to Steam,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.000 --> 00:08:00.640
with the CEO saying “my long term hope regarding&nbsp;
Epic's store were misplaced or overly optimistic.”&nbsp;

00:08:00.640 --> 00:08:06.720
Now we’re starting to uncover part of the answer.
Tim Sweeney keeps yapping about Valve being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.720 --> 00:08:12.400
“unfair to consumers”, then pays publishers&nbsp;
to pull products from other stores,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.400 --> 00:08:18.240
so customers come to the Epic Games Store…
Sure doesn’t sound very “pro consumer”.&nbsp;

00:08:18.240 --> 00:08:22.640
But there’s more.
Most of these third party platforms… suck.&nbsp;

00:08:22.640 --> 00:08:29.600
Ubisoft Connect, the EA App, Amazon Prime Gaming,&nbsp;
they often feel clunky, their selection is much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.600 --> 00:08:35.520
smaller, and people don’t enjoy the experience.
Even Amazon, with their basically infinite money,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.520 --> 00:08:39.120
couldn’t beat Valve, who’s a fraction of the size.
[Ethan Evans, VP of Prime Gaming at Amazon]: We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:44.480
failed multiple times to disrupt the game&nbsp;
platform Steam. We were at least 250x bigger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.480 --> 00:08:51.280
and we tried everything. But ultimately, Goliath&nbsp;
lost. At Amazon, we assumed that size and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.280 --> 00:08:56.720
visibility would be enough to attract customers,&nbsp;
but we underestimated the power of existing user&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.720 --> 00:09:01.920
habits. We never validated our core assumptions&nbsp;
before investing heavily in solutions. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.920 --> 00:09:06.960
truth is that gamers already had the solution to&nbsp;
their problems, and they weren't going to switch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.960 --> 00:09:12.880
platforms just because a new one was available.
We needed to build something dramatically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.880 --> 00:09:18.240
better, but we failed to do so.”
These are so bad, that publishers will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.240 --> 00:09:23.760
force customers to launch games through these&nbsp;
platforms, even if the game is listed on Steam.&nbsp;

00:09:23.760 --> 00:09:28.560
Players… hate these things. Hate them.
We can see more and more,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.560 --> 00:09:33.680
that those who want Valve gone, don’t&nbsp;
actually care about player choice.&nbsp;

00:09:33.680 --> 00:09:39.680
They care about having a bigger slice of the pie.
“the Rothschilds, Microsoft Xbox, Nintendo and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.680 --> 00:09:45.520
Sony PlayStation are the ones that benefit if&nbsp;
Steam goes belly up, we the consumers lose,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.520 --> 00:09:52.160
as the biggest competitor would be eradicated.”
Antitrust protects competition, not consumers.&nbsp;

00:09:52.160 --> 00:09:55.840
Can it lead to better value&nbsp;
for consumers? Absolutely.&nbsp;

00:09:55.840 --> 00:09:59.040
But this is where a lot of&nbsp;
people have become suspicious.

00:09:59.040 --> 00:10:03.120
“If Valve is enforcing price parity,&nbsp;
then that limits real competition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.120 --> 00:10:08.960
and removing that restriction would let other&nbsp;
stores offer lower prices. Ok. But I think people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.960 --> 00:10:14.000
should be realistic about what happens next. This&nbsp;
does not mean publishers will suddenly become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.000 --> 00:10:19.600
generous and start selling $70 games for $50.
Publishers would still use Steam's infrastructure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.600 --> 00:10:24.560
for exposure, wishlists, then tell people&nbsp;
to buy from their own platform for a tiny&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.560 --> 00:10:31.360
discount or some exclusive digital items. Maybe&nbsp;
the game is $5 cheaper, or maybe it is the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.360 --> 00:10:37.040
price but comes with bonus skins or currency.”
I don’t think many of these people and companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.040 --> 00:10:42.720
care much about the free market and consumers.
Many of them, want Valve out of the way.&nbsp;

00:10:42.720 --> 00:10:47.600
But, there’s still a bigger question.
Why won’t players leave Steam for Epic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.600 --> 00:10:52.960
even when given games for free?
Why do people trust Valve? Even if they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.960 --> 00:11:06.480
seem to be preventing lower prices?
We need a bit more context.&nbsp;

00:11:06.480 --> 00:11:11.440
In 1998, Valve released their&nbsp;
very first game. Half Life.&nbsp;

00:11:11.440 --> 00:11:16.640
One of the most revered video games ever made.
From Valve came many games with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.640 --> 00:11:22.160
incredible quality and polish.
Portal 1 and 2, Team Fortress one and 2,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.160 --> 00:11:29.520
Counter-strike, along with sequels Source, Global&nbsp;
Offensive and 2, Left 4 Dead one and two, Dota 2.&nbsp;

00:11:29.520 --> 00:11:34.240
Note that many of these were first made&nbsp;
by fans as mods, who were hired by Valve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.240 --> 00:11:38.960
to flesh out these projects.
So why does this matter?&nbsp;

00:11:38.960 --> 00:11:42.640
For one important reason.
Steam was built, and is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.640 --> 00:12:07.360
owned by people who are their customers.
Steam isn’t a public company, it’s private.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.360 --> 00:12:13.920
And the majority is owned by Gabe Newell.
Originally, in 2003, Steam was just a platform&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.920 --> 00:12:19.680
to sell Valve’s games, then in 2005, it&nbsp;
became a home for third party titles.&nbsp;

00:12:19.680 --> 00:12:23.920
The Epic Games store didn’t&nbsp;
launch till 13 years later.&nbsp;

00:12:23.920 --> 00:12:31.360
Epic is also private, but the majority is owned by&nbsp;
large conglomerates: Tencent, Disney, Sony, etc.&nbsp;

00:12:31.360 --> 00:12:36.720
“I don't know how many people remember this, but&nbsp;
Steam practically rescued PC gaming in the late&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.720 --> 00:12:44.000
'00s. The 7th gen was a nightmare for PC gaming;&nbsp;
gaming was in the clutches of the big publishers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.000 --> 00:12:50.560
who were focusing almost exclusively on consoles.&nbsp;
Steam centralized the market and started the sales&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.560 --> 00:12:56.560
and in record time, the market shifted.
Steam democratized video game releases.&nbsp;

00:12:56.560 --> 00:13:02.400
Steam won, because it was great, and part of&nbsp;
that is Valve’s impeccable customer support.&nbsp;

00:13:02.400 --> 00:13:07.280
Play a game and find it sucks? Or maybe you&nbsp;
just don’t like it? If you bought it less&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.280 --> 00:13:13.280
than two weeks ago, and played under two hours,&nbsp;
you’ll get a full refund, no questions asked.&nbsp;

00:13:13.280 --> 00:13:18.080
Not all platforms are this generous.
It’s not just Steam games either.&nbsp;

00:13:18.080 --> 00:13:22.880
There are stories upon stories of Valve&nbsp;
customer support going above and beyond.&nbsp;

00:13:22.880 --> 00:13:27.680
“Steam replaced my LCD Deck for free&nbsp;
months after purchase because I told&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.680 --> 00:13:34.320
them it crashed while playing Ark Survival as&nbsp;
well as Spider-Man, then got stuck at 400mhz.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:34.320 --> 00:13:38.960
They wouldn’t even take no for an answer,&nbsp;
immediately wanted to replace it for me.”&nbsp;

00:13:38.960 --> 00:13:44.080
Then there’s family sharing. Share your library&nbsp;
with someone else, who can play your games,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.080 --> 00:13:49.360
for free, without buying them. As long as&nbsp;
you aren’t using them at the same time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:49.360 --> 00:13:54.800
This first released in 2013.
All this goes beyond gaming too.&nbsp;

00:13:54.800 --> 00:14:00.000
They have their own operating system,&nbsp;
SteamOS, which is free, and Linux-based.&nbsp;

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:04.560
This is why a SteamDeck or Steam&nbsp;
Machine also functions as a PC.&nbsp;

00:14:04.560 --> 00:14:08.320
Steam also strongly encourages&nbsp;
regional pricing in emerging markets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.320 --> 00:14:14.560
like Brazil, Russia, or Southeast Asia.
Steam's recommended price is 40–70% below&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:14.560 --> 00:14:20.160
the USD price, so they try to reflect what&nbsp;
that game would cost relative to local income.&nbsp;

00:14:20.160 --> 00:14:25.120
Steam even penalizes games in discovery&nbsp;
if they don’t apply these prices.&nbsp;

00:14:25.120 --> 00:14:30.400
Steam has also banned in-game advertising&nbsp;
“If your game's business model relies on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:30.400 --> 00:14:35.920
advertising on other platforms, you will need to&nbsp;
remove those elements before shipping on Steam”.&nbsp;

00:14:35.920 --> 00:14:40.240
Steam also forces developers to&nbsp;
disclose uses of AI in development.&nbsp;

00:14:40.240 --> 00:14:44.800
Which of course, Tim Sweeney finds upsetting…
[Tim Sweeney]: “I think it's really irresponsible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:44.800 --> 00:14:50.720
of Valve. They shouldn't do it, because it makes&nbsp;
it much, much, much harder for a game developer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:50.720 --> 00:14:54.320
to have a chance of success”
Sure Tim.&nbsp;

00:14:54.320 --> 00:15:00.960
Examples on top of examples of features that might&nbsp;
annoy big companies, but are very pro-consumer.&nbsp;

00:15:00.960 --> 00:15:07.760
Even beyond all these examples, if you search “Why&nbsp;
is Steam / Gabe Newell beloved” you’ll find long,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:07.760 --> 00:15:11.520
detailed posts and stories.
Their practices are great,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:11.520 --> 00:15:16.960
but Valve has decades of goodwill. From&nbsp;
making games to supporting those games.&nbsp;

00:15:16.960 --> 00:15:22.560
For example, their massive prize pools for&nbsp;
esports tournaments, like Counterstrike or Dota 2.&nbsp;

00:15:22.560 --> 00:15:33.360
And then, there’s Gabe Newell.
People trust Valve,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:33.360 --> 00:17:00.240
because they trust the people at the top.
His email has been public for years, and he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:00.240 --> 00:17:06.320
consistently replies to customers, about anything.
He’s been pretty open and honest with customers.&nbsp;

00:17:06.320 --> 00:17:09.760
Valve is one of the highest&nbsp;
paying companies in the world,&nbsp;

00:17:09.760 --> 00:17:12.800
But also, makes some of the&nbsp;
most revenue per employee,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:12.800 --> 00:17:19.680
and seem to pay those employees very well.
And let me be clear: Valve is a for profit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:19.680 --> 00:17:24.640
company. They are out to make money.&nbsp;
But they understand the most important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:24.640 --> 00:17:27.520
part of the equation.
Customer service.&nbsp;

00:17:27.520 --> 00:17:32.720
Is Valve perfect? No.
A lot of these lawsuits are still ongoing.&nbsp;

00:17:32.720 --> 00:17:36.240
Steam might be doing something&nbsp;
wrong by fixing horizontal prices,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:36.240 --> 00:17:43.440
and forcing the same prices everywhere, but I’m&nbsp;
unsure if this will turn into cheaper prices.&nbsp;

00:17:43.440 --> 00:17:47.600
I don’t think Steam is a normal monopoly.
[Mat Piscatella]: Market share isn't what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:47.600 --> 00:17:52.720
defines a monopoly, folks. A monopoly is where&nbsp;
there's a single seller for a particular service&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:52.720 --> 00:17:57.120
or good with no substitutes and the ability&nbsp;
to charge excessively high rates because of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:57.120 --> 00:18:03.520
this. Anyone can launch a PC distribution&nbsp;
service, and there have been A LOT of them.&nbsp;

00:18:03.520 --> 00:18:08.800
But, I sure trust Valve a heck of a lot&nbsp;
more than I do Tim Sweeney and Epic Games.&nbsp;

00:18:08.800 --> 00:18:14.640
In a world of so many greedy companies, I&nbsp;
don’t mind a company like Valve having control.&nbsp;

00:18:14.640 --> 00:18:18.480
Because they got there by&nbsp;
offering the best service.&nbsp;

